.TH LOCKD 1 "September 2026" "lockd 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lockd \- modular Linux security hardening tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lockd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lockd applies, simulates and verifies security hardening on Linux systems
through declarative, reversible modules. Every change can be previewed
with a real dry-run and rolled back afterwards; the reported state comes
from the real system, not from a cached file.
.PP
Without arguments, \fBlockd\fR opens the GUI when a display is available,
or the interactive TUI otherwise.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Run the security audit and print a 0\(en100 score with suggestions.
.TP
.B list
List available modules (optionally filtered by \fB\-\-category\fR).
.TP
.B status
Show the current state of each module reconciled with the real system.
.TP
.BI "enable " module_id
Enable a module.
.TP
.BI "disable " module_id
Disable a module.
.TP
.BI "simulate " module_id
Preview what enabling a module would do, without changing anything.
.TP
.BI "info " module_id
Show detailed information about a module.
.TP
.B profiles
List available hardening profiles.
.TP
.BI "profile " profile_id
Apply a hardening profile.
.TP
.B levels
List cumulative hardening levels (basic, advanced, expert, paranoid).
.TP
.BI "level " level_id
Apply a hardening level.
.TP
.B advanced
Open the interactive TUI (works over SSH and in headless environments).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Simulate everything. No changes are made to the system.
.TP
.BI "\-\-log\-level " LEVEL
Set the logging level (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/lockd/modules/
Hardening modules (shell scripts + metadata).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/lockd/profiles/
Hardening profiles (YAML).
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/lockd/lockd-helper
Privileged helper invoked via Polkit for all root operations.
.TP
.I /var/lib/lockd/journal.jsonl
Append\-only journal of every privileged operation.
.TP
.I ~/.config/lockd/state.json
Per\-user module state, reconciled against the real system on startup.
